Answer: Even numbers from 1 to 10 can be printed directly using separate print statements:

```java
System.out.println(2);
System.out.println(4);
System.out.println(6);
System.out.println(8);
System.out.println(10);
```

However, this approach creates redundant code.

Answer: A for loop and the modulus operator can be used:

```java
for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
if (i % 2 == 0) {
System.out.print(i + " ");
}
}
```

The loop checks every number from 1 to 10. If the remainder after division by 2 is zero, the number is even and is printed.

Answer: The loop can start from 2 and increment by 2:

```java
for (int i = 2; i <= 10; i += 2) {
System.out.print(i + " ");
}
```

The output is: 2 4 6 8 10 Because the value increases by 2 each time, every value generated by the loop is even.

Answer: Odd numbers can be printed by starting from 1 and increasing the value by 2:

```java
for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i += 2) {
System.out.print(i + " ");
}
```

The output is: 1 3 5 7 9 Starting from 1 and adding 2 always produces odd numbers.

Answer: An odd number gives a remainder of 1 when divided by 2. Therefore, the following condition can be used:

```java
if (i % 2 != 0) {
System.out.print(i + " ");
}
```

A number is odd when it is not completely divisible by 2.

