Answer: The loop does not need to check all the numbers up to n - 1. It can check only up to n / 2:

```java
boolean isPrime(int n) {
for (int i = 2; i <= n / 2; i++) {
if (n % i == 0) {
return false;
}
}
return true;
}
```

Any number greater than n / 2 cannot divide n completely, except for n itself. Since the loop starts from 2, checking beyond n / 2 is unnecessary.

Answer: The isPrime method can be reused for every number in the range:

```java
System.out.println("Enter a number:");
int n = scan.nextInt();
NumberManipulation nm = new NumberManipulation();
for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
boolean result = nm.isPrime(i);
if (result) {
System.out.print(i + " ");
}
}
```

If the input is 10, the output is: 2 3 5 7 The loop checks each number individually and prints only the numbers for which isPrime returns true.

Answer: A counter variable can be initialized to zero. Whenever a prime number is found, the counter is incremented:

```java
int count = 0;
for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
if (nm.isPrime(i)) {
count++;
}
}
System.out.println("Number of prime numbers from 1 to " + n + " is " + count);
```

For example, from 1 to 100, there are 25 prime numbers.

