For prime numbers from 1 to a user-provided value:

```java
for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
if (nm.isPrime(i)) {
System.out.print(i + " ");
}
}
```

The same logic works for both fixed and user-defined ranges.

Answer: Each iteration performs these three operations:

```java
int lastDigit = n % 10;
reverse = reverse * 10 + lastDigit;
n = n / 10;
```

Their purposes are: n % 10 accesses the last digit. reverse * 10 + lastDigit adds the digit to the reversed number. n / 10 removes the last digit from the original number.

Answer: The method can return an integer containing the reversed value:

```java
class NumberManipulation {
int reverseNumber(int n) {
int reverse = 0;
while (n != 0) {
int lastDigit = n % 10;
reverse = reverse * 10 + lastDigit;
n = n / 10;
}
return reverse;
}
}
```

Answer: The method can be called using an object of the class:

```java
